Fix this in ui.html at text-segment collection, in segStyle and the loop that calls it. code.js does not change.

1. Detect a sup/sub run. Walk from r.el up to the paragraph's block element. A run is SUP if any element on that path meets one of these:
   - its tagName is SUP;
   - its cs.verticalAlign is 'super' or 'text-top', or a positive length/percentage;
   - its cs.position is 'relative' and parseFloat(cs.top) < 0 (this is Tailwind preflight).
   A run is SUB under the mirror conditions: tagName SUB; verticalAlign 'sub' or a negative value; or position relative with parseFloat(cs.bottom) < 0 or parseFloat(cs.top) > 0.
   Store the result as style.shift = 'SUP' | 'SUB' | null. Include it in the key so the run is not merged with its neighbours.

2. Use Unicode when the whole text fits. If every character of the run is in the map, replace the text with the Unicode superscript or subscript characters. Then set the segment's size, lineHeight and letterSpacing from the parent block's computed style rather than the shrunken element's, because these glyphs are already small and raised or lowered.
   - Superscript map: 0⁰ 1¹ 2² 3³ 4⁴ 5⁵ 6⁶ 7⁷ 8⁸ 9⁹ +⁺ -⁻ −⁻ =⁼ (⁽ )⁾ nⁿ iⁱ
   - Subscript map: 0₀ 1₁ 2₂ 3₃ 4₄ 5₅ 6₆ 7₇ 8₈ 9₉ +₊ -₋ −₋ =₌ (₍ )₎
   - Text that is already ™ or ® passes through unchanged.
   The result is "H₂O", "πr²" and "¹" in Figma, with real raised/lowered glyphs. The layer is still editable and auto layout still works. Inter, Roboto and most Google UI fonts include these glyphs; where one is missing, Figma substitutes a fallback glyph, which still beats a baseline digit.

3. Otherwise keep the reduced size, as today. This covers sup text with letters or brackets, such as '[2]' or 'TM' written as text. Set the segment lineHeight to null (inherit the paragraph's line height) instead of the shrunken px value, so the run never changes the line box. Do not try to position a separate overlay TEXT.

